<body>
    <header>
        <h1>Global Intentional Homicide Trends</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">A Multi-Dimensional Analysis (1990–2023)</p>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem; opacity: 0.8;">Source: UNODC Crime Trends Survey (CTS) | Policy Analyst Brief</p>
    </header>

    <div class="kpi-dashboard">
        <div class="kpi-card highlight">
            <div class="kpi-value">1.84</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Global Median Homicide Rate (2023)</div>
            <div style="font-size: 0.75rem; color: #718096; margin-top: 0.5rem;">per 100,000 population</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card highlight">
            <div class="kpi-value"><span class="ratio-badge">3.2x</span></div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Male-to-Female Homicide Ratio</div>
            <div style="font-size: 0.75rem; color: #718096; margin-top: 0.5rem;">Males are 3.2x more likely victims</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">64.2</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Highest National Rate (2023)</div>
            <div style="font-size: 0.75rem; color: #718096; margin-top: 0.5rem;">Saint Kitts and Nevis</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card success">
            <div class="kpi-value">-5.3%</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Decade-over-Decade Change</div>
            <div style="font-size: 0.75rem; color: #718096; margin-top: 0.5rem;">2000s to 2010s average</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">84</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Countries Reporting (2023)</div>
            <div style="font-size: 0.75rem; color: #718096; margin-top: 0.5rem;">Down from peak of 162 in 2008</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container">
        <!-- Executive Summary -->
        <section id="executive-summary">
            <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Key Finding:</strong> The world is becoming <em>moderately safer</em> from intentional homicide. Global average rates declined from 8.24 per 100,000 in the 1990s to 7.80 in the 2020s—a 5.3% improvement over three decades. However, this aggregate progress masks significant regional disparities and demographic inequalities.
            </div>
            <div class="warning-box">
                <strong>Critical Disparity:</strong> Males face a homicide rate 3.2 times higher than females globally. Young adults aged 25-29 experience the highest victimization rates, with a median of 3.99 per 100,000—making targeted youth violence prevention programs essential.
            </div>
            <p>The Caribbean and Central America continue to bear a disproportionate burden, with Saint Kitts and Nevis (64.2), Saint Vincent and the Grenadines (51.3), and Jamaica (49.4) recording rates 20-30 times higher than safest nations like Singapore (0.07) or Japan (0.23).</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Global Overview -->
        <section id="global-overview">
            <h2>Global Homicide Rate Overview</h2>
            <p>The UNODC data reveals a complex picture of global homicide trends spanning 1990–2023. The dataset encompasses 201 countries and territories with varying reporting periods, creating an unbalanced panel that requires careful interpretation.</p>

            <h3>Global Trend Trajectory (1990–2023)</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="globalTrendChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <div class="two-column">
                <div>
                    <h3>Decade Averages</h3>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Decade</th><th>Mean Rate</th><th>Change</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>1990s</td><td>8.24</td><td>—</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>2000s</td><td>8.52</td><td style="color: var(--accent);">+3.4%</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>2010s</td><td>7.67</td><td style="color: var(--success);">-10.0%</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>2020s</td><td>7.80</td><td style="color: var(--accent);">+1.7%</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3>Key Observations</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Peak reporting occurred in 2008 with 162 countries submitting data</li>
                        <li>2023 shows lowest median rate (1.84) but also lowest coverage (84 countries)</li>
                        <li>The 2010s represented the safest decade on record since 1990</li>
                        <li>Mean rates remain higher than median due to high-outlier nations</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Regional Comparison -->
        <section id="regional-comparison">
            <h2>Regional & Country-Level Comparison</h2>
            <p>Homicide rates vary dramatically across countries and regions. The Caribbean and parts of Central America experience rates orders of magnitude higher than East Asia and Western Europe.</p>

            <div class="two-column">
                <div>
                    <h3>Top 10 Highest Homicide Rates (2023)</h3>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Country</th><th>Rate per 100K</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody id="top10Table"></tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3>Top 10 Lowest Homicide Rates (2023)</h3>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Country</th><th>Rate per 100K</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody id="bottom10Table"></tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Interpretation:</strong> The disparity between highest (Saint Kitts and Nevis: 64.16) and lowest (Holy See: 0.00) represents a ratio exceeding 640:1. Even among reported nations, the gap between safest and most dangerous exceeds 900-fold.
            </div>

            <h3>Regional Distribution (Most Recent Year)</h3>
            <div class="chart-container small">
                <canvas id="regionalChart"></canvas>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Sex Disparity -->
        <section id="sex-disparity">
            <h2>Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>
            <p>Gender is one of the strongest predictors of homicide victimization risk. Males face substantially higher rates of homicide victimization across virtually all countries and time periods.</p>

            <div class="insight-box" style="text-align: center; padding: 1.5rem;">
                <span class="ratio-badge" style="font-size: 2rem; padding: 0.5rem 1.5rem;">3.2x</span>
                <p style="margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 1.1rem;">Global Male-to-Female Homicide Rate Ratio (2023)</p>
                <p style="color: #718096;">Males are more than three times as likely to be homicide victims</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Male vs. Female Homicide Rates Over Time</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="genderTrendChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <h3>Male-to-Female Ratio Trend</h3>
            <div class="chart-container small">
                <canvas id="ratioTrendChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <div class="two-column">
                <div>
                    <h3>What Drives Male Vulnerability?</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Young male adults (15-44) represent highest-risk demographic</li>
                        <li>Male victimization often linked to gang violence and armed conflict</li>
                        <li>Females more likely killed by intimate partners</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3>Implications for Policy</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Gender-specific prevention strategies needed</li>
                        <li>Youth violence interventions should target young males</li>
                        <li>Intimate partner violence remains a female-specific concern</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Age Group Analysis -->
        <section id="age-group-analysis">
            <h2>Age Group Analysis</h2>
            <p>Homicide risk varies significantly across age groups, with young adults bearing the heaviest burden globally. The following analysis uses sex-disaggregated data where age-group breakdowns were available.</p>

            <h3>Average Homicide Rate by Age Group (Males)</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="ageGroupChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Age Group</th><th>Mean Rate</th><th>Median Rate</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody id="ageGroupTable"></tbody>
            </table>

            <div class="warning-box">
                <strong>Key Finding:</strong> Adults aged 25-29 experience the highest median homicide rate (3.99 per 100,000) among all age groups. The 18-29 age cohort consistently shows elevated risk across countries, underscoring the need for targeted youth violence prevention programs.
            </div>

            <p><strong>Note:</strong> Age-group-specific data is only available for male and female separately; combined "both" sex data provides only total population rates (age_group = "ALL").</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Notable Trends -->
        <section id="notable-trends">
            <h2>Notable Country-Level Trends & Outliers</h2>
            <p>Beyond global patterns, individual countries have experienced dramatic changes in homicide rates over the observation period. Below we highlight countries with the most significant improvements and deteriorations.</p>

            <div class="two-column">
                <div>
                    <h3 style="color: var(--success);">Most Improved Countries</h3>
                    <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; color: #718096;">Countries with largest absolute decrease (minimum 5-year span, positive starting rate)</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Country</th><th>Period</th><th>Change</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody id="improversTable"></tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 style="color: var(--accent);">Most Deteriorated Countries</h3>
                    <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; color: #718096;">Countries with largest absolute increase (minimum 5-year span)</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Country</th><th>Period</th><th>Change</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody id="deterioratorsTable"></tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="limitations-box">
                <strong>Statistical Note:</strong> Countries with zero homicides recorded in their starting year were excluded from trend calculations to avoid division-by-zero artifacts. Some small island nations and territories show extreme percentage changes due to low baseline numbers.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Data Limitations -->
        <section id="data-limitations-recommendations">
            <h2>Data Limitations & Policy Recommendations</h2>

            <div class="limitations-box">
                <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Unbalanced Panel:</strong> Not all 201 countries report data for every year. Coverage peaked at 162 countries in 2008 but dropped to 84 in 2023.</li>
                    <li><strong>Missing Data:</strong> Years with no available data are simply absent from the dataset—no interpolation was performed.</li>
                    <li><strong>UK Caveat:</strong> United Kingdom data covers England and Wales only, not Scotland and Northern Ireland.</li>
                    <li><strong>Territorial Variations:</strong> Some territories (Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico) are reported separately from parent countries.</li>
                    <li><strong>Age Group Data:</strong> Disaggregated age data exists only for male/female separately, not for combined "both" sex.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Strengthen Data Collection:</strong> Improve national reporting capacity to enable better trend monitoring and cross-country comparisons.</li>
                <li><strong>Target High-Risk Demographics:</strong> Young males aged 18-29 should be priority beneficiaries of violence prevention programs.</li>
                <li><strong>Address Regional Hotspots:</strong> The Caribbean and Central America require sustained attention and international cooperation to address persistently high rates.</li>
                <li><strong>Learn from Success Stories:</strong> Countries showing significant improvements (e.g., Colombia's reduction from 70+ to ~25 per 100,000) offer models worth studying.</li>
                <li><strong>Gender-Responsive Approaches:</strong> Prevention strategies should account for the 3.2x male-to-female disparity and the female-specific risk of intimate partner violence.</li>
            </ol>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Conclusion:</strong> While global homicide rates show modest long-term improvement, the persistent gap between safest and most dangerous nations—along with stark gender and age disparities—demands continued policy attention and international cooperation.
            </div>
        </section>

        <footer>
            <p><strong>Data Source:</strong> UNODC Crime Trends Survey (CTS) via Kaggle dataset</p>
            <p><strong>Report Generated:</strong> 2026 | Analysis covers 1990–2023</p>
            <p><strong>Methodology:</strong> All statistics derived directly from provided dataset; no imputation or external sources used.</p>
        </footer>
    </div>

    <script>
